<GuessTheWord />

## A machine that plays the guessing game

In 1951, Claude Shannon ran an experiment that looks a lot like this one. He showed people the
start of a passage and asked them to guess the next letter, again and again, and used how well they
did to measure how predictable written English is. The answer: very.<Cite id="shannon1951" />

A large language model plays the same game. The model above is GPT-2 small, which OpenAI released
in 2019.<Cite id="radford2019" /><Cite id="gpt2-model-card" /> It read the words *The cat sat on
the* and gave every possible continuation a chance. Notice that it doesn't commit to one answer.
It spreads its bets: *floor*, *bed*, and *couch* are all likely, and no single option gets even
one chance in ten.

## Over and over

One guess is only one word. To write a sentence, the model adds its pick to the end of the text and
plays the game again with the longer text. Step through the first few rounds:

<RecordedGeneration name="the-cat-sat" title="The guessing game, repeated" maxSteps={4} />

That's the whole trick, repeated: guess, pick, add, repeat. Everything else in this site explains
how the guessing works, and how much can be built on top of it.

<RecapCard>

- A large language model is a machine that predicts the next piece of text.
- It doesn't pick one answer. It gives every possible continuation a probability.
- To write more, it adds a pick to the text and predicts again, over and over.

</RecapCard>

